  <p>
    The distribution of Oscar wins across decades reveals a fascinating rhythm of consolidation and dispersion. 
    The <strong>1940s stand out as the most diverse decade</strong>, with 12 unique composers across just 10 ceremonies 
    — a reflection of Hollywood's studio-system depth, where rotating music departments and a deep bench of 
    European émigré talent kept the field wide open. By contrast, the <strong>1950s saw the tightest concentration</strong>: 
    only 7 unique composers won across 10 years, with Dimitri Tiomkin alone claiming three of those wins.
  </p>
  <div class="chart-container">
