    <em>Jaws</em> and <em>Star Wars</em>, fundamentally changing how audiences perceived film music. 
    <strong>Maurice Jarre</strong> brought exotic orchestral colors to epics like <em>Lawrence of Arabia</em>. 
    <strong>John Barry</strong> established his signature sound with <em>Born Free</em>. The era also welcomed 
    pop sensibilities — <strong>Burt Bacharach</strong> won for <em>Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid</em> (1969), 
    and <strong>Marvin Hamlisch</strong> for <em>The Way We Were</em> (1973). With 18 unique composers across 
    20 ceremonies, the field remained open but stars were forming.
  </p>

  <h3><span class="era-badge era-modern">Modern · 1980–1999</span></h3>
  <p>
    The Modern era was the age of synthesis — both musical and technological. <strong>Alan Menken</strong> 
    dominated with three wins for Disney's animated renaissance. <strong>John Williams</strong> continued his 
    reign with <em>E.T.</em> and <em>Schindler's List</em>. But the era also made room for radical new voices: 
    <strong>Vangelis</strong> brought electronic minimalism to <em>Chariots of Fire</em> (1981), 
    <strong>Giorgio Moroder</strong> won for <em>Midnight Express</em> (1978, overlapping into this era's cultural 
    influence), and <strong>Hans Zimmer</strong> introduced a new sonic palette with <em>The Lion King</em> (1994). 
    The 18 unique composers across 20 ceremonies suggest a healthy balance between established giants and emerging innovators.
  </p>

  <h3><span class="era-badge era-contemporary">Contemporary · 2000–2026</span></h3>
  <p>
    The Contemporary era is the most concentrated in Oscar history. With only 22 unique composers across 
    27 ceremonies, repeat winners have become the norm rather than the exception. <strong>Ludwig Göransson's</strong> 
    four wins in eight years represent a level of dominance unseen since the Golden Age. <strong>Howard Shore</strong> 
    bookended the era's first half with his <em>Lord of the Rings</em> scores, while <strong>Alexandre Desplat</strong> 
